theory.intro:
To graph a piecewise-defined function, sketch each subfunction only on its own interval. At each boundary, work out the value and mark it with a **filled circle** if included or an **open circle** if excluded. The finished graph gives the domain, range and parity.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-0.8,0) -- (6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.6) -- (0,5.3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick, <-] (-0.6,0.45) -- (2.5,2.5) node[pos=0.4, above left] {$y=g(x)$};
\draw[thick, ->] (2.5,3.8) -- (5.6,4.7) node[pos=0.6, below right] {$y=h(x)$};
\fill (2.5,2.5) circle (2.5pt);
\draw[fill=white] (2.5,3.8) circle (2.5pt);
\draw (2.5,0.12) -- (2.5,-0.12);
\node[below] at (2.5,-0.12) {$a$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
The first branch is an increasing line segment from $(-3,0)$, included, to $(1,4)$, excluded. The second branch is a decreasing line segment from $(1,4)$ to $(5,0)$, with both endpoints included. The filled point at $(1,4)$ completes the join.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[->] (-3.7,0)--(5.7,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.6)--(0,4.8) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick] (-3,0)--(1,4)--(5,0);
\fill (-3,0) circle (2pt);
\fill (1,4) circle (2pt);
\fill (5,0) circle (2pt);
\node[below left] at (-3,0) {$(-3,0)$};
\node[above right] at (1,4) {$(1,4)$};
\node[below right] at (5,0) {$(5,0)$};
\node[below left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (0,0) {$0$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The first branch gives $[0,4)$ and the second gives $[0,4]$. Their union is $[0,4]$, so the range is $[0,4]$.

For $x<1$, sketch the downward-opening parabola with vertex $(0,5)$. At the boundary, $5-1^2=4$, so mark an open circle at $(1,4)$.
For $x\ge1$, sketch the increasing line. Since $1+2=3$, mark a filled point at $(1,3)$. The unequal boundary heights give a jump at $x=1$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=11mm,y=7mm,draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[->] (-3,0)--(3.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2)--(0,6.2) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,<-,domain=-2.5:1,samples=70,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{5-\x*\x});
\draw[thick,->] (1,3)--(3.5,5.5);
\draw[fill=white] (1,4) circle (2.5pt);
\fill (1,3) circle (2.5pt);
\node[above left] at (0,5) {$(0,5)$};
\node[right] at (1,4.1) {$(1,4)$};
\node[right] at (1,2.8) {$(1,3)$};
\node[below left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (0,0) {$0$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
The parabola gives $(-\infty,5]$: its vertex is included and it extends downward without bound. The line gives $[3,\infty)$. These intervals overlap, so the range is $(-\infty,\infty)$.

Sketch the decreasing line only for $x<0$, the horizontal segment from $(0,2)$ to $(3,2)$, and the increasing line only for $x>3$.
At both boundaries the adjacent branches have height $2$. The middle branch includes both endpoints, so use filled points at $(0,2)$ and $(3,2)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=9mm,y=9mm,draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[->] (-2.5,0)--(5.7,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.5)--(0,4.8) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,<-] (-2,4)--(0,2);
\draw[thick] (0,2)--(3,2);
\draw[thick,->] (3,2)--(5,4);
\fill (0,2) circle (2pt);
\fill (3,2) circle (2pt);
\node[below left] at (0,2) {$(0,2)$};
\node[below right] at (3,2) {$(3,2)$};
\node[below left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] at (0,0) {$0$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Each outer branch gives $(2,\infty)$ and the middle branch supplies $2$. The range is $[2,\infty)$.

Sketch $y=-2x^3$ only to the left of the $y$-axis. It passes through $(-1,2)$ and approaches $(0,0)$ with an excluded endpoint.
Sketch $y=2x^3$ only on and to the right of the $y$-axis. It includes $(0,0)$ and passes through $(1,2)$. The filled point at the origin completes the continuous join.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=17mm,y=8mm,draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[->] (-1.8,0)--(1.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.6)--(0,5) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,<-,domain=-1.3:0,samples=60,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{-2*\x*\x*\x});
\draw[thick,->,domain=0:1.3,samples=60,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{2*\x*\x*\x});
\fill (0,0) circle (2pt);
\node[left] at (-1,2) {$(-1,2)$};
\node[right] at (1,2) {$(1,2)$};
\node[below left] at (0,0) {$(0,0)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The left line ends at an open circle at $(-4,-1)$. The parabola includes its endpoints $(-2,5)$ and $(1,2)$ and its vertex $(0,1)$. The right line starts at an open circle at $(8,6)$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=5mm,y=6mm,draw=black,text=black,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw[->] (-6.8,0)--(10.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-3.5)--(0,9) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,<-] (-6,-3)--(-4,-1);
\draw[fill=white] (-4,-1) circle (2.5pt);
\draw[thick,domain=-2:1,samples=70,smooth,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{\x*\x+1});
\fill (-2,5) circle (2pt);
\fill (1,2) circle (2pt);
\draw[thick,->] (8,6)--(10,8);
\draw[fill=white] (8,6) circle (2.5pt);
\node[above left] at (-4,-1) {$(-4,-1)$};
\node[above left] at (-2,5) {$(-2,5)$};
\node[below left] at (0,1) {$(0,1)$};
\node[right] at (1,2) {$(1,2)$};
\node[below right] at (8,6) {$(8,6)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
For $x<-4$, the outputs satisfy $y<-1$, giving $(-\infty,-1)$.
On $[-2,1]$, the parabola has minimum $1$ at its vertex. Its endpoint heights are $5$ and $2$, so its output interval is $[1,5]$.
For $x>8$, the outputs satisfy $y>6$, giving $(6,\infty)$.
The range is $(-\infty,-1)\cup[1,5]\cup(6,\infty)$. Keep the gaps between these output intervals.

